<resources>
    <string name="app_name">My Habit App</string>

    <!--
      The Notion connection wizard (M4.12).

      Two rules govern every string below. Nothing names a Notion property — CLAUDE.md §3.4 makes
      the schema user data, so "Date" and "📖 Read" appear only because Notion said so at runtime.
      And every failure line names the fix rather than the fault: the user cannot act on "404".
    -->
    <string name="setup_step_counter">Step %1$d of %2$d</string>
    <string name="setup_back">Back</string>

    <!-- Step 1 — the token -->
    <string name="setup_token_title">Connect to Notion</string>
    <!--
      ⭐⭐ M12.9, third sentence, and it is the one this screen was missing. CLAUDE.md §3.6 calls the
      token a password, and this is the only moment the app asks for one — yet it said nothing about
      where the password goes. The answer already existed and was written well (about_data_token),
      but About lives INSIDE the graph the setup gate wraps, so a first-run user cannot reach it:
      the explanation was readable only after the credential had already been handed over.

      Deliberately shorter than about_data_token rather than a copy of it. This is a first screen
      somebody wants to get past; the full statement — logs, backups, the single host — is still on
      About for anyone who goes looking.
    -->
    <string name="setup_token_body">Your habits live in a Notion database. Paste a token so this app can read and update it. It is stored encrypted on this device and sent only to Notion.</string>
    <string name="setup_token_label">NOTION TOKEN</string>
    <string name="setup_token_placeholder">ntn_…</string>
    <!--
      The wording of this path was confirmed against the live docs in M4.1 (docs/notion-api.md §3).
      Notion renamed "internal integration" to "personal access token" and "integrations" to
      "connections" — sending the user to look for the old names sends them looking for nothing.

      ⚠ M12.9 re-checked this against the live docs and could NOT reproduce the "shown once" quote:
      two pages do not mention it, and one says an *internal connection* token can be retrieved from
      the ••• menu. M4.1 captured a direct quote ("You won't be able to see it again") for personal
      access tokens specifically, which is stronger than two pages being silent — so the line stays.
      Being over-cautious here costs a user nothing; being wrong the other way loses them a token.
    -->
    <string name="setup_token_helper">In Notion: Developer portal → Personal access tokens → New token. Notion shows it once, so copy it before closing.</string>
    <string name="setup_token_continue">Continue</string>

    <!-- Step 2 — which database -->
    <string name="setup_database_title">Pick your habit database</string>
    <string name="setup_database_body">These are the databases your token can reach.</string>
    <string name="setup_database_untitled">Untitled database</string>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ M12.3, and it is CLAUDE.md §3.3's trap said out loud on the screen where a user meets it.
      A valid token that has been shared with nothing returns a SUCCESSFUL search with zero results
      — not an error — so no failure banner fires and the picker simply had nothing to draw. The
      user saw "These are the databases your token can reach" above an empty space, which reads as
      a broken app rather than as an unfinished step in Notion.

      Continue is already correctly disabled here (canContinueFromDatabase), so this is not a dead
      end — what was missing was the sentence saying what to do. The second half is the wizard's own
      documented finding: Notion's search is eventually consistent, so a database shared a moment
      ago can be absent from this list and perfectly reachable by the link field directly below.
    -->
    <string name="setup_database_empty">No databases yet. In Notion: open your habit database → ••• → Add connections, and add your connection. One shared a moment ago can take a minute to appear here — its link works straight away.</string>
    <string name="setup_database_continue">Continue</string>
    <string name="setup_database_use_link">Don\'t see it? Paste a link instead</string>
    <string name="setup_link_label">DATABASE LINK</string>
    <string name="setup_link_placeholder">https://www.notion.so/…</string>
    <string name="setup_link_helper">In Notion: open the database → ••• → Copy link.</string>
    <string name="setup_link_submit">Find it</string>
    <string name="setup_link_unreadable">That doesn\'t look like a Notion link. Copy the database\'s link from Notion and paste the whole thing.</string>

