      Year view (M8.3) - docs/design-spec.md §4.

      Two strings, and the screen has no others: its title is screen_title_year, its card's second
      line is habit_card_summary, and there is no sub-nav to label because the design gives this
      view no paging at all.

      percent_value is a template rather than a bare "%d%%" concatenation so a locale that writes
      its percent sign first, or with a space, can say so. It is shared with the Stats screen's
      "completion rate" tile (M9.4) rather than duplicated: one percentage, one place to fix.

      The eyebrow is uppercase IN THE STRING rather than through a text transform: it is a word a
      translator may need to case differently, and Turkish alone is reason enough not to let code
      decide what a capital letter is.
    -->
    <string name="percent_value">%1$d%%</string>
    <string name="year_completion_eyebrow">COMPLETION</string>
    <!--
      M13.2 - the same two facts, in the order an ear needs them.
      The eyebrow is drawn BELOW the figure, and reading order is drawing order, so a screen
      reader announced "87%" and only then "COMPLETION" - a value ahead of what it measures.
      Lower case because this is spoken, never drawn: the eyebrow above is uppercase for the
      eye, and an all-caps content description is read out letter by letter by some engines.
    -->
    <string name="year_completion_a11y">%1$s completion</string>

    <!--
      Stats view (M9) - docs/design-spec.md §5.

      The eyebrow is uppercase IN THE STRING, for the reason the Year block above gives: casing is a
      translator's decision, and Turkish alone is reason enough not to let code decide what a
      capital letter is.

      There is deliberately no string for the grid itself. YearDotGrid clears its own semantics
      (ADR-089): a screen reader cannot usefully be read 365 coloured squares, and the four stat
      tiles beside it say the same thing in words.
    -->
    <string name="stats_year_overview_eyebrow">YEAR OVERVIEW</string>

    <!--
      The four stat tiles - docs/design-spec.md §5.3. Labels are sentence case, as the design draws
      them; the tile renders them at 10sp in white/30, which is why they are short.

      stats_count_value looks pointless and is not: routing a bare number through a resource is what
      makes it render in the locale's own digits. A Kotlin Int.toString() would print ASCII digits
      beside percentages that had been localised, which is the kind of mismatch nobody spots until a
      translator does.

      ⚠ The values JITTER, and that is the faithful port. ADR-021 read DM Sans 4.004's tables out of
      the bundled binary: it has no tnum feature at all, and its 1 is 342 units against 0 at 656. We
      request tnum anyway so the intent is recorded and it starts working the day the font gains it.
      The prototype's own tabular-nums is equally inert.
    -->
    <string name="stats_tile_completed_days">Completed days</string>
    <string name="stats_tile_completion_rate">Completion rate</string>
    <string name="stats_tile_current_streak">Current streak</string>
    <string name="stats_tile_best_streak">Best streak</string>
    <string name="stats_count_value">%1$d</string>

    <!--
      The monthly completions chart - docs/design-spec.md §5.4. The eyebrow is uppercase in the
      string, for the reason the two blocks above give.

      The other three build the chart's contentDescription, and it is worth saying why this canvas
      speaks when YearDotGrid's does not: a year is thirteen buckets here rather than 365 squares,
      and no other element on the screen prints a per-month figure at all. Clearing the semantics
      without replacing them would delete the only place that number appears.

      stats_chart_month_separator is quoted so the XML parser keeps its trailing space. It is
      punctuation rather than copy, and it is a resource because not every language joins a list
      with a comma.
    -->
    <string name="stats_monthly_eyebrow">MONTHLY COMPLETIONS</string>
    <string name="stats_chart_description">Monthly completions: %1$s</string>
    <string name="stats_chart_month">%1$s %2$d</string>
    <string name="stats_chart_month_separator">", "</string>

