      ⭐ THE HEADING IS THE DELIVERABLE, not the tiles. Stats is a per-habit screen: everything above
      this heading answers for the habit selected at the top, and these two do not change whatever
      is selected. The user chose "their own section with a heading" over "two more tiles in the
      grid" with both shapes drawn for them, precisely so that the change of scope is legible
      instead of reading as a tile that has stopped working.

      ⚠ stats_measure_window NAMES THE WINDOW, and it has to. Thirty days is the only span on this
      screen that is not the trailing year the grid and the chart use, so a label reading just
      "Weight" would let the reader carry the year down from the section above. The number comes
      from GetMeasureAverages.MEASURE_WINDOW_DAYS rather than being typed here, so a label that
      said 30 while the use case computed 60 is not expressible.

      ⚠⚠ stats_measure_none is a DASH, not a zero. A window with no readings has no average, and
      "0.0 kg" would be a confident, specific, wrong number where the honest answer is that the user
      has not weighed themselves this month. That distinction runs from the storage layer - no row
      for a day with no reading - all the way to this string.

      stats_measure_value keeps one decimal: Weight arrives as 84 and displays as 84.00 in Notion
      (docs/notion-schema.md §2.4), and Sleep is decimal hours where 7.5 means 7h30m. One place is
      enough to tell 84.2 from 84, and two would imply a precision a mean of a dozen readings has
      not got. The unit is separate because it is blank for a measure the user gave none.
    -->
    <string name="stats_measures_eyebrow">DAILY AVERAGES</string>
    <string name="stats_measure_window">%1$s · last %2$d days</string>
    <string name="stats_measure_value">%1$.1f</string>
    <string name="stats_measure_value_with_unit">%1$.1f %2$s</string>
    <string name="stats_measure_none">—</string>
    <plurals name="stats_measure_readings">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d reading</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d readings</item>
    </plurals>

    <!--
      M9.6. Shown INSIDE the chart card, in place of the drawing, for a habit too young to have a
      curve - a line needs two points, so one month has nothing to draw.

      The card is kept and the sentence goes inside it rather than the card being removed. Two
      reasons: a section that appears from nowhere a month later is a screen the user cannot learn,
      and this was measured - M9.6 rendered the alternatives on a device and a lone month drawn as a
      4dp dot reads as a chart that failed rather than as one month of history.

      It states when the chart arrives rather than what is missing, and it is not a warning: a young
      habit is a normal condition, and CLAUDE.md §1 rules out nagging about those.
    -->
    <string name="stats_chart_not_yet">The chart starts once this habit has two months of history.</string>

    <!--
      The manage-habits screen (M7.5). UNDESIGNED — the prototype has no such screen at all.

      Two facts do all the work here, and both are ADR-010's: a habit IS a checkbox column in the
      user's Notion database, and the colour is the one thing that is ours. So the copy says where
      the name and the history live, and says that the colour does not go there. There is no "add a
      habit" call to action and there never will be.

      ⚠ What this copy deliberately does NOT promise: that a column added in Notion becomes a
      habit. It does not — only the saved mapping decides (M7.4/ADR-079), because Weight, Sleep,
      Area and Progress share the same database. M7.8 builds the route that adopts a new column and
      owns the sentence that describes it.

      habits_notion_pending is the disabled button's helper. It leads with what happens next rather
      than with what is missing, which is the wording the user chose for sync_waiting_for_network.
    -->
    <string name="habits_body">Each of these is a checkbox column in your Notion database, so its name and its history live there.</string>
    <!--
      M15.5. One sentence covering both app-local controls on this screen rather than two, because
      they are the same claim: the order and the colours are ours, and Notion never sees either.
      Notion has no order property at all (docs/notion-schema.md §3), so there is nothing there for
      this to disagree with.
    -->
    <string name="habits_colour_body">Tap a colour to change how a habit looks, or the arrows to reorder the list. Both stay on this phone — Notion never sees them.</string>
    <!--
      Named per habit, so five rows of two buttons are ten distinguishable stops for a screen
      reader rather than ten identical "Move up"s. Passed as onClickLabel, so TalkBack reads it as
      the ACTION on an already-named row ("double tap to move Read up") instead of renaming the row.
      The disabled state comes from clickable(enabled = false) and needs no string of its own.
    -->
    <string name="habits_move_up">Move %1$s up</string>
    <string name="habits_move_down">Move %1$s down</string>
    <string name="habits_open_in_notion">Open in Notion</string>
    <string name="habits_notion_pending">Available after the next sync.</string>
    <string name="habits_empty">No habits yet. They arrive from Notion with the first sync.</string>
    <!--
      M7.8's sentence, which ADR-079 said this screen owed and could not yet say: a new checkbox
      column is not a habit until the mapping names it, because Weight, Sleep, Area and Progress
      live in the same database (docs/notion-schema.md §4.3).
    -->
    <string name="habits_mapping_body">Added a column in Notion? It becomes a habit once you pick it here. Columns like Weight and Sleep stay out of the way.</string>
    <string name="mapping_edit_action">Choose which columns are habits</string>

    <!--
      M12.7's row on the sync screen, and mapping_edit_action's twin: the way to repair one half of
      the connection without destroying the other. Shown only when the failure is token-shaped.
    -->
    <string name="token_replace_action">Paste a new token</string>
    <string name="habits_colour_blue">Blue</string>
    <string name="habits_colour_green">Green</string>
    <string name="habits_colour_orange">Orange</string>
    <string name="habits_colour_purple">Purple</string>
    <string name="habits_colour_red">Red</string>

    <!--
      Undo snackbar (M6.6) — UNDESIGNED. The prototype has no snackbar because it has no click
      handlers at all (docs/design-spec.md §0), so this copy is ours. The user read it at M6.11 and
      kept the wording; what they changed was WHEN it fires, not what it says.

      The sentence names the HABIT and the DAY on purpose. The flipped pill already says that
      something happened; what it cannot say is that the tap landed on Thursday when Wednesday was
      meant, or on Reading when Running was — the two mistakes seven 44dp pills across five stacked
      cards actually invite. Naming both is the whole reason the message exists; "Done" or "Saved"
      would be the nagging-about-a-normal-condition this feature is already at risk of.

      There is no "today" variant any more, and the absence is the decision: M6.11 made a tap on
      today SILENT, so %2$s is always a real date. A "today" string would be a resource nothing can
      reach. See WeekViewModel.habitToAnnounce.
    -->
    <string name="week_undo_checked">%1$s checked off for %2$s</string>
    <string name="week_undo_cleared">%1$s cleared for %2$s</string>
    <string name="week_undo_action">Undo</string>

    <!--
      M13.12. The pill does not move when a write fails - the Week screen draws from Room with no
      optimistic overlay - so this snackbar is the only thing that says anything happened. It leads
      with what did not happen and offers the fix, and unlike the undo copy it is shown for today's
      column too: M6.11's silence is about a tap the ring makes obvious, not about a write that
      never landed.
    -->
    <string name="week_save_failed_checked">Couldn\'t check off %1$s for %2$s</string>
    <string name="week_save_failed_cleared">Couldn\'t clear %1$s for %2$s</string>
    <!-- No habit on screen to name - impossible from a gesture, and never silent regardless. -->
    <string name="week_save_failed_unnamed">Couldn\'t save that change</string>
    <string name="week_save_failed_action">Retry</string>

