      The About screen (M11.8) — UNDESIGNED. The prototype has no such view at all
      (docs/design-spec.md), so every word here is ours.

      ⚠⚠ This screen exists to say TRUE things, which is why the copy is specific rather than
      reassuring. CLAUDE.md §9.12 records that the old "all data stays on your device" line is now
      FALSE — habit data is transmitted to Notion at the user's instruction — and M14.6's Play
      Data Safety form inherits whatever is written here. A vague sentence on this screen becomes a
      wrong declaration on that form.
    -->
    <string name="about_version">Version %1$s (%2$d)</string>

    <!--
      What leaves the device. The order is deliberate: where the data lives, then what a tap
      actually does, then the one host, then the token, then what disconnecting destroys.

      "Your habits live in your Notion workspace" leads because it is the thing that makes the rest
      true — this app is a client for a database the user already owns, and the copy on the phone
      is the mirror, not the original.
    -->
    <string name="about_data_eyebrow">YOUR DATA</string>
    <string name="about_data_home">Your habits live in your Notion workspace. This app keeps a copy on this device so it works with no signal, and syncs changes both ways whenever it can reach Notion.</string>
    <string name="about_data_offline">Checking a habit off is saved on this device first and sent to Notion afterwards. Nothing you do here waits for the network.</string>
    <string name="about_data_one_host">api.notion.com is the only server this app ever contacts. There is no account to create, no server of ours, and no analytics or crash reporting of any kind.</string>
    <string name="about_data_token">Your Notion token is stored encrypted on this device. It leaves only as the authorisation header on a request to Notion, and is never written to a log or a backup.</string>
    <!--
      The last line is the one a user is most likely to be looking for, and the second sentence is
      the reassurance that belongs next to it: this app archives nothing and deletes nothing in
      Notion (ADR-036 — v1 has no delete at all), so disconnecting can only ever destroy the local
      mirror.
    -->
    <string name="about_data_disconnect">Disconnecting deletes the token from this device, and can delete the local copy with it. Nothing in your Notion workspace is ever deleted by this app.</string>

